**Claim 1.**

A light-emitting diode device comprising:

a substrate having a first surface and a second surface opposite to the first surface;

a semiconductor stack disposed on the first surface of the substrate, the semiconductor stack including a first semiconductor layer of a first conductivity type, an active layer disposed on the first semiconductor layer, and a second semiconductor layer of a second conductivity type disposed on the active layer, wherein the second conductivity type is opposite to the first conductivity type;

a first electrode electrically connected to the first semiconductor layer and a second electrode electrically connected to the second semiconductor layer, the first electrode and the second electrode being laterally spaced apart from each other;

a first conductive connecting structure electrically coupled to the first electrode, the first conductive connecting structure including a first main portion and a plurality of first extending parts protruding laterally from the first main portion; and

a second conductive connecting structure electrically coupled to the second electrode, the second conductive connecting structure including a second main portion and a plurality of second extending parts protruding laterally from the second main portion;

wherein the plurality of first extending parts of the first conductive connecting structure and the plurality of second extending parts of the second conductive connecting structure are arranged asymmetrically with respect to a central axis of the semiconductor stack, such that at least one of the first extending parts has a first length measured from the first main portion that differs from a second length of at least one of the second extending parts measured from the second main portion; and

wherein the first extending parts and the second extending parts are interdigitated and mutually spaced apart so as to define, over the semiconductor stack, a non-uniform current spreading region in which a current density distributed by the extending parts of shorter length is different from a current density distributed by the extending parts of longer length.

---

**Optional dependent-style elaborations** (to support the independent claim, if desired):

- wherein each of the plurality of first extending parts has a length different from every other of the plurality of first extending parts, the lengths progressively increasing or decreasing along a direction transverse to the central axis;

- wherein the first main portion and the second main portion are disposed on opposite peripheral sides of the semiconductor stack, and the first and second extending parts extend toward each other across an emission area of the active layer;

- wherein the asymmetric arrangement of the extending parts is configured to compensate for a non-uniform current path resulting from a lateral offset between the first electrode and the second electrode, thereby improving luminous uniformity across the emission area.

Would you like me to tailor the claim to a flip-chip configuration, a wire-bond configuration, or a specific application (e.g., micro-LED display, high-power lighting)? I can also draft the accompanying dependent claims and a brief specification summary.
